.Sh DESCRIPTION
.In bmi.h
includes
.In asm.h ,
which selects the native vector width and defines the vector and block
pointer types used across the library.
All functions on this page are
.Ql const
or
.Ql pure
and have no side effects.
.Ss Vector width selection
.In asm.h
inspects the compiler's target macros and defines
.Dv XFT_HAS_512_VEC ,
.Dv XFT_HAS_256_VEC
and
.Dv XFT_HAS_128_VEC
to 0 or 1, and
.Dv XFT_NVEC_SIZE
to the native vector width in bytes:
.Bl -column "AVX512 BW + VL + DQ" "512" "256" "128" "NVEC"
.It Sy Target Ta Sy 512 Ta Sy 256 Ta Sy 128 Ta Sy NVEC
.It AVX512 BW + VL + DQ Ta 1 Ta 1 Ta 1 Ta 64
.It __AVX2__ Ta 0 Ta 1 Ta 1 Ta 32
.It __SSE2__ or __ARM_NEON Ta 0 Ta 0 Ta 1 Ta 16
.It none of the above Ta 0 Ta 0 Ta 0 Ta 8
.El
.Pp
AVX-512F alone is not enough for the 512-bit path: byte- and word-granular
masks need BW, the 128- and 256-bit masked forms need VL, and dword and
qword sign masks need DQ.
A CPU without all three uses the AVX2 path.
All three are part of
.Fl march Ns = Ns Cm x86-64-v4 .
AVX512VBMI2, which byte and word compaction use, is not; only the 8- and
16-bit
.Fn xft_vcompress
forms check it, and fall back to the scalar loop without it.
.Pp
The native types follow the selected width:
.Bl -tag -width "t_nmask"
.It Vt t_nvec
unaligned vector of
.Dv XFT_NVEC_SIZE
.Vt t_u8
lanes:
.Vt t_vu512 ,
.Vt t_vu256 ,
.Vt t_vu128 ,
or an 8-byte vector.
.It Vt t_nveca
the same vector, aligned to its size.
.It Vt t_nmask
an integer with one bit per lane:
.Vt t_u64a ,
.Vt t_u32a ,
.Vt t_u16a
or
.Vt t_u8 .
.It Vt t_nvec16 , t_nvec32 , t_nvec64
unaligned vectors of
.Dv XFT_NVEC_SIZE
bytes with
.Vt t_u16 ,
.Vt t_u32
or
.Vt t_u64
lanes.
.Vt t_nvec16a ,
.Vt t_nvec32a
and
.Vt t_nvec64a
are the aligned forms.
.It Vt t_nmask16 , t_nmask32 , t_nmask64
the mask of the matching lane width, one bit per lane:
.Vt t_u32a ,
.Vt t_u16a
or
.Vt t_u8
depending on the lane count
.Pq Dv XFT_NVEC_SIZE No / lane size .
.El
.Pp
The fixed-width vector types are always defined regardless of target:
.Vt t_vu128 ,
.Vt t_vu256
and
.Vt t_vu512
have
.Vt t_u8
lanes and alignment 1;
.Vt t_vu128a ,
.Vt t_vu256a
and
.Vt t_vu512a
are aligned to their size.
.Vt t_vu Ns Ar W Ns _ Ns Ar S
are
.Ar S Ns -bit
vectors of
.Ar W Ns -bit
lanes with alignment 1, for
.Ar W
in 16, 32 and 64 and
.Ar S
in 128, 256 and 512, for example
.Vt t_vu32_256 ;
the
.Vt a
suffix
.Pq Vt t_vu32_256a
gives the form aligned to its size.
All vector types are
.Ql may_alias .
.Pp
The block pointer types are
.Ql restrict
and
.Ql const
pointers named
.Vt t_blk Ns Ar W Ns Ar M ,
where
.Ar W
is 8, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512 or
.Sq n
(native vector) and
.Ar M
is
.Sq w
(writable pointee) or
.Sq r
(const pointee).
A trailing
.Sq a ,
as in
.Vt t_blk64ra
or
.Vt t_blknwa ,
points to the aligned variant of the element type.
.Vt t_blk8w
and
.Vt t_blk8r
have no aligned form.
.Ss Bit counting
.Fn xft_popcount_u32
and
.Fn xft_popcount_u64
return the number of set bits in
.Fa x .
.Pp
.Fn xft_memctz_u16
through
.Fn xft_memctz_u128
return the number of trailing zero bits in
.Fa x ;
.Fn xft_memclz_u16
through
.Fn xft_memclz_u128
return the number of leading zero bits.
A zero argument returns the width of the type in bits.
.Pp
On x86_64,
.Fn xft_popcount_*
use
.Ql popcnt ,
and the
.Fn xft_memctz_*
and
.Fn xft_memclz_*
families use
.Ql tzcnt
and
.Ql lzcnt
when the target defines
.Dv __BMI__ .
On aarch64 they use
.Ql clz ,
.Ql rbit
and, with NEON,
.Ql cnt .
Elsewhere a branchless portable version is used.
.Ss Byte order
.Fn xft_bswap16 ,
.Fn xft_bswap32
and
.Fn xft_bswap64
reverse the byte order of
.Fa x .
.Pp
.Fn xft_to_be*
and
.Fn xft_to_le*
convert a host-order value to big- or little-endian order;
.Fn xft_from_be*
and
.Fn xft_from_le*
convert back to host order.
Each is either the identity or the matching
.Fn xft_bswap* ,
chosen at compile time from
.Dv __BYTE_ORDER__ .
.Ss Scalar helpers
.Fn xft_max_s ,
.Fn xft_maxu8 ,
.Fn xft_maxu32 ,
.Fn xft_maxu64
and
.Fn xft_maxu128
return the larger of two unsigned values without a branch.
.Pp
.Fn xft_tern
returns
.Fa value1
if
.Fa cond
is 1 and
.Fa value2
if it is 0, using masks rather than a branch.
.Fn xft_dtern
does the same on the bit patterns of two
.Vt t_f64
values.
.Fa cond
must be exactly 0 or 1.
.Pp
.Fn xft_next_pow2
returns the smallest power of two greater than or equal to
.Fa qword .
.Fn xft_last_pow2
returns the largest power of two less than or equal to
.Fa qword .
.Pp
.Fn xft_align_fwd_integer
rounds
.Fa n
up to a multiple of
.Fa align ;
.Fn xft_align_bwd_integer
rounds it down.
.Fa align
must be a power of two.
.Pp
.Fn xft_roll_mask
returns
.Ql ~0 << (chunk_size - n) ,
a mask with the low
.Fa chunk_size
\-
.Fa n
bits clear.
.Pp
.Fn xft_rotl64
rotates
.Fa hash
left by
.Fa n
bits.
.Pp
.Fn xft_hasz64
is nonzero if any byte of
.Fa x
is zero.
The lowest set bit is the high bit of the lowest zero byte;
bits above it may be set spuriously.
.Pp
.Fn xft_populate
returns
.Fa y
replicated into all eight bytes of a 64-bit word.
.Ss Masked loads
.Fn xft_vmask_load128 ,
.Fn xft_vmask_load256
and
.Fn xft_vmask_load512
load a vector from
.Fa ptr ,
which need not be aligned.
Byte
.Va i
of the result is the byte at
.Fa ptr Ns Va [i]
if bit
.Va i
of
.Fa mask
is set, and zero otherwise.
.Fn xft_vmask_load
does the same at the native width.
They are declared only when the matching
.Dv XFT_HAS_*_VEC
macro is 1;
.Fn xft_vmask_load
is always declared.
.Pp
With
.Dv XFT_HAS_512_VEC
all three widths use the AVX-512 masked byte load, which does not access
unselected bytes.
Otherwise the 128- and 256-bit loads return zero without reading memory if
.Fa mask
is 0.
If a full-width load from
.Fa ptr
would cross a 4096-byte page, they instead load the two aligned chunks
containing the lowest and highest selected bytes; otherwise they do one
full-width load.
Unselected bytes may be read, but never from a page that holds no selected
byte, so the load does not fault where reading only the selected bytes
would not.
Without any SIMD support,
.Fn xft_vmask_load
reads only the selected bytes.
These functions are built without AddressSanitizer instrumentation.
.Ss Internal vector primitives
The following functions are used by
.Xr xft_mem 3 ,
.Xr xft_cstr 3 ,
.Xr xft_swissmap 3
and
.Xr xft_indexmap 3 .
They are declared in the private header
.Pa src/internal/xft_p_asm.h ,
which is not installed.
Each has a 128-, 256- and 512-bit form
.Pf ( Fn xft_vsplat128
and so on) declared under the matching
.Dv XFT_HAS_*_VEC ,
and a native-width form on
.Vt t_nvec
and
.Vt t_nmask ,
declared in
.In bmi.h .
The functions below operate on byte lanes; see
.Sx Wider lanes
for the 16-, 32- and 64-bit forms.
.Bl -tag -width "xft_vrangemask(v, lo, hi)"
.It Fn xft_viota
a vector whose lane
.Va i
is
.Va i .
.It Fn xft_vsplat b
a vector with every byte set to
.Fa b .
.It Fn xft_vmask vec
a mask with bit
.Va i
set to the high bit of byte
.Va i .
Compiles to
.Ql pmovmskb
(128 and 256 bits),
.Ql vpmovb2m
(512 bits),
or a NEON sequence on aarch64.
.It Fn xft_veqmask vec b
bit
.Va i
set if byte
.Va i
equals
.Fa b .
.It Fn xft_vtestmask vec b
bit
.Va i
set if byte
.Va i
and
.Fa b
have a set bit in common.
.It Fn xft_vrangemask v lo hi
bit
.Va i
set if
.Fa lo
\(<= byte
.Va i
\(<=
.Fa hi ,
compared unsigned.
.It Fn xft_vcompress vec mask
the bytes of
.Fa vec
selected by
.Fa mask ,
packed in order into the low lanes, with the remaining lanes zero.
Only the sized forms exist.
With
.Dv XFT_HAS_512_VEC
and
.Dv __AVX512VBMI2__
it uses
.Ql vpcompressb ;
otherwise every form uses a scalar loop.
.El
.Ss Wider lanes
Every primitive above has a 16-, 32- and 64-bit lane form, with
.Va W
the lane width in bits.
The native-width forms are
.Fn xft_vsplat<W> ,
.Fn xft_vmask<W> ,
.Fn xft_veqmask<W> ,
.Fn xft_vtestmask<W> ,
.Fn xft_vrangemask<W> ,
.Fn xft_vcompress<W> ,
.Fn xft_vmask_load<W>
and
.Fn xft_viota<W> ,
on
.Vt t_nvec<W> ,
.Vt t_nvec<W>a
and
.Vt t_nmask<W> ,
with
.Va W
one of 16, 32 or 64.
The sized forms are named
.Fn xft_v<op><S>_<W> ,
for example
.Fn xft_vsplat256_32 ,
with
.Va S
one of 128, 256 or 512 and are declared in the private header
.Pa src/internal/xft_p_asm.h
under the matching
.Dv XFT_HAS_*_VEC .
.Pp
They behave like the byte forms with lanes of
.Va W
bits, with these differences:
.Bl -bullet
.It
The scalar arguments
.Fa b ,
.Fa lo
and
.Fa hi
have the lane type
.Pf ( Vt t_u16 ,
.Vt t_u32
or
.Vt t_u64 ) .
.It
.Fn xft_vmask<W>
sets bit
.Va i
to the sign bit of lane
.Va i .
.It
.Fn xft_vrangemask<W>
compares lanes unsigned.
.It
.Fn xft_vmask_load<W>
loads whole lanes: lane
.Va i
of the result is the lane at
.Fa ptr
if bit
.Va i
of
.Fa mask
is set, and zero otherwise.
.It
.Fn xft_vcompress<W>
packs the selected lanes into the low lanes.
.El
.Pp
With
.Dv XFT_HAS_512_VEC
every width uses a single AVX-512 builtin per operation, including the
masked loads and
.Ql vpcompress{d,q} ,
and
.Ql vpcompressw
when
.Dv __AVX512VBMI2__
is also defined.
Without it:
.Bl -bullet
.It
masks come from
.Ql movmskps ,
.Ql movmskpd
or a signed pack followed by
.Ql pmovmskb ;
comparisons and splats use vector operators;
.It
the 32- and 64-bit masked loads use
.Ql vpmaskmovd
and
.Ql vpmaskmovq
when
.Dv XFT_HAS_256_VEC
is set, which do not fault on unselected lanes;
.It
the 16-bit masked loads, the 128-bit loads without AVX2 and the
native-width fallback use a branch-free load in which every unselected lane
reads the lowest selected lane and is then cleared.
They return zero without reading memory if
.Fa mask
is 0, and never read outside the selected lanes' addresses;
.It
compaction is an iterative loop that moves the lowest set lane each
iteration; the 8- and 16-bit forms also use it with
.Dv XFT_HAS_512_VEC
when
.Dv __AVX512VBMI2__
is not defined.
.El
.Pp
When none of
.Dv XFT_HAS_128_VEC ,
.Dv XFT_HAS_256_VEC
and
.Dv XFT_HAS_512_VEC
is set, the native-width forms are a plain lane loop.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Every function returns the value described above.
None of them fail or set an error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Iterate over the set bits of a mask and read a big-endian length:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

t_size	sum_selected(const t_u64 *vals, t_u64 mask)
{
	t_size	sum;

	sum = 0;
	while (mask)
	{
		sum += vals[xft_memctz_u64(mask)];
		mask &= mask - 1;
	}
	return (sum);
}

t_u32	read_be32(const t_u8 *hdr)
{
	return (xft_from_be32(*(const t_u32 *)hdr));
}
.Ed
.Pp
Load the first
.Fa n
bytes of a buffer, with
.Fa n
less than
.Dv XFT_NVEC_SIZE ,
without reading past a page boundary:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
t_nvec	v;

v = xft_vmask_load(ptr, (t_nmask)(~(-1ULL << n)));
.Ed
